.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
This library is a set of routines for creation, deletion and verification
of regular UNIX files, UNIX directories, UNIX hard links, and UNIX symbolic 
links.

.LP
.B file()
checks a given UNIX path and verifies the existence of the path and
that the path
points to a regular UNIX file with no hard links made to the file.
If all of these conditions hold then 
.B file()
returns a value of TRUE, otherwise a FALSE value is returned.
If the stat_buf is not NULL then the results of an lstat() on the
UNIX path are returned in this buffer.  Note that EOVERFLOW errors
are ignored ont the lstat().

.LP
.B file_mk()
creates a regular UNIX file at the given path. Any parent directories
that do not exist are automatically created. If the file already
exists and is a file
then the existing file's length is truncated to 0, it is emptied.  
If the file is created
.B file_mk()
returns a TRUE value and the file descriptor has a positive integer
value.  Otherwise, FALSE is returned.
The mode is the file permissions to be used on any new file
that is created.

.B file_rm()
removes a regular UNIX file.  If the path specified does not point to
a regular UNIX file or if an error occurs while the file is being
removed
then FALSE is returned, otherwise TRUE is returned.

.LP
.B file_dir()
checks a given UNIX path to verify if the path exists and if the path 
points to a UNIX directory.
If all of these conditions hold then 
.B file_dir()
returns a value of TRUE, otherwise a FALSE value is returned.
If the value of stat_buf is not NULL then the results of an lstat() on the
UNIX path are returned in this buffer.  Note that EOVERFLOW errors are
ignored on the lstat().

.LP
.B file_dir_empty()
deletes the contents of the given directory.  All files and subdirectories
are removed.  If the subdirectories are not empty then they are emptied.
This function is equivalent to performing

.B	/bin/rm \fI \-r \-f <path>/\(** <path>/.\(**\fR

A FALSE value is returned by
.B file_dir_empty()
if the path given is not a UNIX directory or if any errors occur during
the removal process.  If the directory is "Emptied" then a TRUE value is
returned.

.LP
.B file_dir_mk()
creates a UNIX directory at the given path. Any parent directories
that do not exist are automatically created.  If the file already
exists of if the directory is successfully created 
then a TRUE value is returned, otherwise FALSE is returned.
The mode is the file permissions to be used on the new directory.

.LP
.B file_dir_rm()
removes a given directory and its contents, if it is not empty.
If there are non-empty subdirectories then they are emptied.
This function is equivalent to performing

.B	/bin/rm \fI \-r \-f <path>

A FALSE value is returned by
.B file_dir_rm
if the path given is not a UNIX directory or if any errors occur during
the removal process.  If the directory is removed then a TRUE value is
returned.

.LP
.B file_link()
checks a given UNIX path to verify if the path exists and if the path 
points to a regular UNIX file which has at least one hard link to the file.
If all of these conditions hold then 
.B file_link()
returns a value of TRUE, otherwise a FALSE value is returned.
If the value of stat_buf is not NULL then the results of an lstat() on the
UNIX path are returned in this buffer.  Note that EOVERFLOW errors are
ignored on the lstat().

.LP
.B file_link_mk()
creates a hard link between an existing file and the given destination 
path. Any parent directories which do not exist in the destination path will
be created automatically. FALSE is returned by
.B file_link_mk
if the source path does not exist or if errors occur when the hard
link is created.  TRUE is returned if the hard link is successfully
created or if it already exists.
The mode is the file permissions to be used on any new directories 
that are created, if any.

.LP
.B file_link_rm()
decrements, by one, the number of hard links to a regular UNIX file 
which has multiple hard links to it.  Once there are no hard links
to the file
.B file_rm()
can be used to remove the file.
If the hard link count is successfully decremented TRUE is returned,
otherwise FALSE is returned.

.LP
.B file_sym()
checks a given UNIX path to verify if the path exists and if the path 
is a symbolic link to another UNIX file.
If all of these conditions hold then 
.B file_sym()
returns TRUE, otherwise a FALSE value is returned.
If the value of stat_buf is not NULL then the results of an lstat() on the
UNIX path are returned in this buffer.  Note that EOVERFLOW errors are
ignored on the lstat().

.LP
.B file_sym_mk()
creates a symbolic link between an existing file and the given 
destination path. Any parent directories which do not exist in the 
destination path will be created automatically. FALSE is returned by
if the source path does not exist or if errors occur when the symbolic
link is created.  TRUE is returned if the symbolic link is successfully
created or if it already exists.
The mode is the file permissions to be used on any new directories 
that are created, if any.

.LP
.B file_sym_rm()
removes the given symbolic link. TRUE is returned if the symbolic link
is successfully removed, otherwise FALSE is returned.

